# Copy to .env and fill in. docker compose refuses to start if the `:?` ones are missing.

# ── secrets (required) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DB_PASSWORD=change-me
# Fleet API keys, "name:key" pairs. This is what every node sends as X-API-Key.
#   openssl rand -hex 16
UPDATE_API_KEYS=fleet-a:2f8c1e9a4b7d6c3e

# ── Keycloak: one account across AeroCoreUpdate, AeroCore and aerotunnel ──────────────────────
# Name the realm ONCE. The issuer and the JWKS endpoint follow from Keycloak's fixed URL
# layout, so they are derived rather than asked for twice. Leave these empty to keep
# everything on local passwords and API keys.
#
# EdDSA ONLY. A realm ships RSA keys by default, and RS256 cannot be verified on an Android
# node (the NDK has no OpenSSL) — so add an EdDSA realm key and set the token signing
# algorithm to EdDSA, or nothing here verifies anything.
KEYCLOAK_BASE_URL=
KEYCLOAK_REALM=
# The host part of KEYCLOAK_BASE_URL when it is a name the container cannot resolve itself —
# typically the machine's mDNS name (`<hostname>.local`), chosen so an IP change breaks
# nothing. Compose maps it to the Docker host. Leave empty when KEYCLOAK_BASE_URL is an IP or
# a name DNS already resolves.
KEYCLOAK_HOST=

# Checked per surface and deliberately different: a token minted for the nodes must not also
# open the admin API. Keycloak omits `aud` unless the client has an audience mapper — add
# one, or every token is refused.
OIDC_AUDIENCE_ADMIN=aeroserver-admin
OIDC_AUDIENCE_FLEET=aerocore

# The realm role an account must hold before any admin route opens for it. The audience says
# which SURFACE a token was minted for; this says what the account behind it may do. Without
# it every realm user — including the `customer` accounts created below — could point `stable`
# at a release and ship firmware to the whole fleet.
#
# Create this role in the realm and grant it to your operators. A realm that lacks it refuses
# every Keycloak sign-in, which locks every operator out of this server — severe, and
# meant to be: this server holds no other way in.
OIDC_ADMIN_ROLE=aeroserver-admin
# Below it, two roles that are NOT the same power. A publisher uploads artifacts and creates
# releases; a viewer only reads. Moving a channel — the single action that reaches an
# aircraft — belongs to the admin role alone, so the person who builds a release and the
# person who approves shipping it can be different people. Empty disables a level.
OIDC_PUBLISHER_ROLE=aeroserver-publisher
OIDC_VIEWER_ROLE=aeroserver-viewer

# The same question one surface down: which realm role may use the FLEET API. The audience
# only says a token was minted for the nodes — in a realm shared with other products, every
# account can ask for one, and with self-registration on that is anyone at all.
#
# Held by the node service account as much as by a person pulling a build. Empty = no role
# check, the historical behaviour. Turning it on is a cutover: grant the role to the nodes
# FIRST, or the whole fleet gets 403 together.
OIDC_FLEET_ROLE=
# The lesser fleet role: may check and download, but only what the `stable` channel points at
# right now — enforced on the download route too, so asking by version number does not get
# around it. Holding the full role as well wins; that is a superset, not a conflict.
OIDC_FLEET_STABLE_ROLE=aerocore-fleet-stable
# Lets a restart taken during an IdP outage still verify tokens.
OIDC_JWKS_CACHE_FILE=keys/jwks-cache.json

# "Sign in with Keycloak" on the admin sign-in page. Without these the page offers the local
# password form only, and a Keycloak account can reach the admin API only by presenting a
# token it obtained some other way.
#
# A CONFIDENTIAL client (Client authentication ON) with the admin UI's address in its valid
# redirect URIs — https://<this server>:9443/* . The secret stays in this process: the code
# exchange is done server-side, which is not a preference but the only thing that works, since
# a browser will not let an HTTPS page talk to an HTTP Keycloak.
# With these set and Keycloak answering, the sign-in page stops asking and simply goes there:
# an operator who is not signed in has nothing else to do on it. Two ways back to the local
# password form remain, and both matter — https://<server>:9443/admin/login.html?local=1, and
# the page itself, which stays put whenever Keycloak does not answer.
OIDC_WEB_CLIENT_ID=
OIDC_WEB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── the admin UI bundle (build-time) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The two above are the SERVER's confidential client, used by the older sign-in page. The
# replacement UI signs in from the browser instead, as a PUBLIC client doing PKCE — no secret
# to hide, which is the only reason the redirect dance can leave this process at all.
#
# This is the issuer the BROWSER dials. Behind a gateway it is the public URL while
# KEYCLOAK_BASE_URL above may be an internal one; a browser can only use the public one.
# Read by Vite at build time, so changing it means rebuilding the image, not restarting it.
# Empty builds a UI that cannot sign anyone in, because this server holds no account.
WEBUI_OIDC_ISSUER=
# A PUBLIC client on that realm whose redirect URIs include https://<this server>/admin/callback,
# carrying an audience mapper for OIDC_AUDIENCE_ADMIN and a realm-roles mapper. Without both,
# every token it issues is refused here.
WEBUI_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=aeroserver-webui

# Only for a split-horizon deployment: behind a reverse proxy the `iss` in a token is the
# PUBLIC URL while this server dials Keycloak internally. Leave both empty otherwise.
#OIDC_ISSUER=
#OIDC_JWKS_URI=

# How /api/v1/* authenticates: apikey | both | jwt
# `both` is the migration state — a node holding a token uses it, one that does not keeps
# working on its API key. Moving to `jwt` is a CUTOVER: every node must already hold a token
# or the whole fleet stops updating at once.
FLEET_AUTH_MODE=apikey

# ── Keycloak admin API (the "Create account" button) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Uses KEYCLOAK_BASE_URL / KEYCLOAK_REALM above. The service account behind these must hold
# `manage-users` and NOTHING more. Never `realm-admin`: this credential lives on this box,
# so anything it can do, an attacker who takes this box can do.
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_CLIENT_ID=
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Realm role granted to an account created from the admin UI. `customer` deliberately: the
# lowest privilege level, never a convenient one. The role must already exist in the realm,
# and the service account also needs `view-realm` to look it up. Empty grants nothing.
KEYCLOAK_DEFAULT_ROLE=customer

# ── self-service sign-up ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Adds a "Create account" form to the sign-in page, usable with no credential at all.
#
# OFF by default, and think before turning it on: the account is a KEYCLOAK account, so it
# signs in to AeroCore and aerotunnel the moment it exists. What keeps it harmless HERE is
# OIDC_ADMIN_ROLE — a self-registered account gets KEYCLOAK_DEFAULT_ROLE and nothing else, so
# it can sign in to Keycloak and reach nothing on this server until an operator grants more.
# Turning this on while OIDC_ADMIN_ROLE is unenforced would hand admin to anyone.
#
# Needs KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_CLIENT_ID/_SECRET above: accounts are created through Keycloak.
ALLOW_SELF_REGISTRATION=0
# Ceiling per source address, so an open form cannot be used to fill the realm.
REGISTER_MAX_PER_IP=5
REGISTER_WINDOW_MINUTES=60

# ── TLS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used as CN and a SAN when generating the self-signed certificate on first boot.
TLS_CN=aerocoreupdate
# Every address a client will actually dial. A certificate that does not name the address is
# the usual reason the UI works on localhost but not from another machine, and a node with TLS
# rejects it outright with no way to click through. Comma-separated; bare IPv4 becomes IP:,
# anything else DNS:. Example: 192.168.1.50,aerocoreupdate.lan
TLS_SAN=
# Mount real files over /data/tls/server.{crt,key} for production.
# The certificate is generated once and kept on the volume: change TLS_SAN and you must delete
# /data/tls/server.{crt,key} for it to be regenerated.

# ── optional ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SIGNING_KEY_ID=rtr-ota-2026
# Set to make manifest URLs absolute; empty leaves them relative, which the spec allows.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=
UPLOAD_MAX_BYTES=536870912
# Where a fresh upload lands. Default `beta`: the new build reaches the test group and stable
# keeps serving what it was, until an admin promotes. Set another channel name to land there
# instead, or an empty value to land nowhere at all (staged, confirmed in the web UI).
# Per-upload override: ?channel=stable — but that needs `channel:write`, which only an admin
# holds. An account that may upload and not publish can land on `beta` or nowhere, and asking
# for anything else is refused: uploading fills the catalog, moving a channel reaches an
# aircraft, and they are deliberately different rights.
# This variable does NOT decide who may publish, and setting it to `stable` does not grant
# anyone the right to land there — it would instead refuse the ordinary uploads of every
# account that holds only `artifact:write`.
AUTO_PROMOTE_CHANNEL=beta
# Refuse a bundle whose config payload omits a param an earlier release set. A bundle carries
# only its own payload, so a node that skips releases never receives what they set — and
# reports success anyway. Params an operator locked are skipped by the node, so re-sending them
# every time is safe. Set to 0 to downgrade this to a warning.
REQUIRE_CUMULATIVE_CONFIG=1

# System a bundle is filed under when its release.json names none. Systems are created by an
# admin from the Systems tab, never invented from a bundle, so this one must already exist.
# The migration creates "default" and files everything published before systems under it.
DEFAULT_SYSTEM=default

# Refuse a bundle that carries no config component at all. REQUIRE_CUMULATIVE_CONFIG only
# fires once an earlier release has set something, so a release line that never ships config
# is never caught by it. Turn this on where every release must restate its full config.
# Note: shipping the core's own config/ directory does NOT satisfy this — that path replaces
# every unlocked param on the node instead of the ones you name.
REQUIRE_CONFIG_COMPONENT=0

# Refuse a bundle whose shipped core config would blank update.server_url / api_key or set
# update.enabled=false. Off: AeroCore already keeps a node's own value for any param that node
# has LOCKED, so a fleet provisioned with those fields locked is safe and the server only
# warns. Turn it on if provisioning does not reliably lock them — a node that applies a blank
# server URL can never be reached again.
REFUSE_CONFIG_LIFELINE=0

# An uploaded artifact sits in the catalog until a channel points at it, and /check never
# offers it. But /update/download resolves by version, so anyone holding a fleet API key can
# fetch a staged build by guessing its number. They cannot make a node apply it — that needs a
# signed manifest — but the bytes are readable. Set to 1 to serve only versions a channel
# points at. Cost: an in-flight download 404s the moment a channel moves past that version,
# and a resuming node starts over.
DOWNLOAD_REQUIRES_CHANNEL=0
CHECK_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# The node has no TLS backend (spec section 10). Set to 1 only if an HTTPS-only server cuts
# the fleet off; it opens plaintext HTTP for /api/v1 while /admin still returns 426.
ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_HTTP=0
HTTP_PORT=9099

# Refuse every request except /api/v1/health while publishing.
MAINTENANCE=0

# ── knobs with defaults ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything below has a working default and is here to be FOUND, not to be set. A setting
# nobody knows about may as well not exist, and the alternative to writing it down is somebody
# reading src/config/index.js to discover why a timeout is what it is.

# The Keycloak session this server holds on a browser's behalf. ACCESS_TTL_SECONDS is only a
# fallback for when the realm's token omits `expires_in`; REFRESH_TTL_SECONDS is how long the
# session cookie lives, so shortening it sends operators back to Keycloak more often.
ACCESS_TTL_SECONDS=900
REFRESH_TTL_SECONDS=604800

# Largest JSON body accepted. Artifact uploads do NOT go through this — they stream, and
# UPLOAD_MAX_BYTES is their limit.
BODY_LIMIT=1000000

DB_POOL_MAX=10
# Apply pending migrations at boot. Turn off only where a separate step owns schema changes.
RUN_MIGRATIONS=1
# How long to let in-flight requests finish on shutdown, in ms. A node mid-download is the
# reason this is not zero.
SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=10000
# Retry-After sent while MAINTENANCE is on, in seconds.
MAINTENANCE_RETRY_AFTER=300

# Refuse a platform that is not one of the thirteen the protocol names. Off accepts anything
# shaped like a platform, which is what a fleet with a new target needs before the list moves.
STRICT_PLATFORMS=1

# When a version has no per-platform `slim` artifact, serve the `fleet` bundle covering that
# platform instead. Section 4 tells operators to publish one bundle for every platform, so a
# server without this fallback answers "no update" forever to everyone.
SLIM_FALLBACK_TO_FLEET=1

# ── signing: moving the private key off this box ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Generate a signing key on first boot if none exists. Off in production: a real deployment
# restores from backup rather than quietly minting a new identity the fleet does not trust.
SIGNING_KEY_AUTOGEN=0
# The end state (spec section 10): refuse any artifact arriving unsigned, and hold no private
# key at all. A server with no key cannot lose one, leak one, or silently regenerate one.
SIGNING_REQUIRE_PRESIGNED=0
# Public halves the upload gate verifies a pre-signed artifact against,
# "key_id:base64,key_id2:base64". Public only — they exist so this server can CHECK a
# signature the release pipeline produced, never so it can produce one.
SIGNING_TRUSTED_KEYS=
# The offline root that signs a signing key's certificate. Its private half never touches
# this machine; that is the entire point of the scheme.
SIGNING_ROOT_KEY_ID=
SIGNING_ROOT_PUBLIC_KEY=
